A good enterprise pattern is similar to GitHub Copilot, Perplexity, or Microsoft 365 Copilot—keep the answer readable, but attach inline citation chips exactly where each claim comes from.

For example, if the user asks:

> How do I request production access?



The response could look like this:


---

Response

To request production access, submit an Access Request in ServiceNow and select the appropriate application and environment [1].

The request must be approved by both your Application Owner and your Line Manager before provisioning begins [1][2].

Before access is granted, you must complete all mandatory compliance training, including Information Security and Data Privacy training [2].

Once all approvals are complete, the Infrastructure Support team provisions the access. The standard turnaround time is 1–2 business days [3].


---

Sources

[1] Production Access Process
📂 Space: DEVOPS
📄 Section: Requesting Production Access

[2] Access Governance Policy
📂 Space: SECURITY
📄 Section: Approval Requirements

[3] Infrastructure Support SLA
📂 Space: INFRA
📄 Section: Service Timelines


---

If the user clicks [2], your UI can:

Open the Confluence page.

Scroll directly to the "Approval Requirements" section.

Highlight the exact paragraph used to generate the answer.


Another example

User: Can I deploy directly to production?

Response:

No. Standard releases require CAB approval before production deployment [1].

Emergency deployments are allowed only through the Emergency CAB process and must include a documented business justification [2].

After deployment, teams are required to perform smoke testing and update the release ticket with the validation results [3].


---

Sources

[1] Release Management Process
[2] Emergency Release SOP
[3] Production Deployment Checklist


---

This style has several advantages:

Users immediately see which statements are backed by documentation.

Multiple citations (e.g. [1][2]) show that a statement was synthesized from more than one source.

The response remains clean and conversational, while the citations provide traceability without interrupting the reading experience.
